# Endings: checked in this order on arrival day; the last one has no conditions.
# Each ending is one carrier clause (the fleet's hail vote) times one arrival clause
# (the storm vote: held the plan, waited it out in orbit, or no storm). Every ending
# closes on the first crate. `title` is the heading; see STORY_BIBLE.md.

card end_lights
type ending
cat ending
title Lights On
when flag signal_answered
when flag arrive_hold
text The storm took the prime zone overnight, so the fleet goes down at the backup site, into the brown, radar for height and Cargo 2's transponder for place, answered before entry and pinging on time. Then the ground grants one more command, lights, and it turns on every floodlight it has: a white smudge right where the terrain map says it should be. Seven ships land three kilometres off. Unloading starts at dawn. The first crate out of Cargo 2's hold says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." The first message off the surface goes to a loading bay at Starbase: "Landed. All eight."

card end_through
type ending
cat ending
title Through the Dust
when flag arrive_hold
text Holding the plan means flying the last kilometres blind: radar for height, and for bearing a knock on the old channel every eleven seconds, still asking. Six ships land three kilometres off. The seventh is the one that slewed: upright on the slope, a leg strut folded under it, loaded past anything it was built for. The take-manual saved the crew, not the ship; it will not fly home. Two sols later the first suit out, the one who argued to answer, climbs the rim and answers from two hundred metres. HELLO RX · OK. To Starbase: "Landed. Seven down, six to fly. Found the eighth."

card end_long_orbit
type ending
cat ending
title The Long Orbit
when flag signal_answered
when flag arrive_wait
text Aerocapture, as voted: two passes through the heat instead of one, every tile watched, every tile holding. Then the long orbit. Week three, night pass: seven tablets on seven galley tables, Cargo 2's manifest coming down a line a pass, read aloud in turn. Tonight's line is a spare drill bit, and seven people cheer a drill bit. Someone asks for the next line; the pass ends. Below, the storm's edge finally shows ground. A week later seven ships land, and the rover drives out to floodlights that hold steady. In Cargo 2's hold the first crate says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." To Starbase: "Landed. All eight."

card end_patience
type ending
cat ending
title Patience
when flag arrive_wait
text Aerocapture, as voted. The ground's hold stands, no first handshake without a suit beside it, and from the long orbit the site rises for minutes a day, low behind the crater rim. Week two: ration bars cut in half on seven tables, and the knock later every night, and every night back. The night before the fleet lands, it isn't. The first suit out, the one who argued to answer, reaches it at dawn: floodlights dark, frost on the wing. A battery pack on the service port, and it wakes, slow, and knocks once. The answer goes in by hand, from two metres, inside the tick. HELLO RX · OK. To Starbase: "Landed. All seven. Found the eighth."

card end_friend
type ending
cat ending
title Old Friend
when flag signal_answered
text Seven ships come down in clear air, thirty kilometres from a light that has held steady since the window. On the second sol the rover drives out, the one who set the answer running at the wheel, and stops at the foot of the rim. Cargo 2 stands tilted, frost in the shadow of its legs, dish jammed against its stop. The ground lifts the last condition, and it sends the only thing it has: its manifest, line by line, drill spares first. In its hold the first crate says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." To Starbase: "Landed. All eight."

card end_close
type ending
cat ending
title Close Enough
text Seven ships land in clear air. On the first sol Cargo 3's ice drill snaps its only bit, and the fleet's water margin starts going down a day at a time; the spare is on a written-off manifest, thirty kilometres off. On the third sol the rover drives out, the one who argued to answer at the wheel, and answers the next knock from two hundred metres. The screen shows one line: HELLO RX · OK. Then the floodlights go out, and stay out: two years of asking, and the battery had one answer left. The bit is where the manifest said. To Starbase: "Landed. All seven. Found the eighth, in time."
